  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Express $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
